WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:04.000
- He's an American rapper, record producer
and entrepreneur.

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:08.969
- He is a very successful hip hop artist.

00:00:08.969 --> 00:00:12.240
- He is worth 550 billion dollars.

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:17.570
- He's Jay Z and here are his top ten rules
for success.

00:00:17.570 --> 00:00:20.680
- My advice is just do things that are true
to you.

00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:26.060
Most of the things that I'm involved with
are an extension of being creative.

00:00:26.060 --> 00:00:28.189
Rocawear is a clothing company.

00:00:28.189 --> 00:00:34.190
It's part of who you are and hip hop is your
attitude and what you're trying to express,

00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:35.850
how you dress.

00:00:35.850 --> 00:00:37.890
I loved sports growing up.

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:46.539
I grew up in a household where sports was
on 24/7 so these are all things that are comfortable

00:00:46.539 --> 00:00:47.539
for me.

00:00:47.539 --> 00:00:49.010
These are things that I like.

00:00:49.010 --> 00:00:54.750
So I would just say get involved in things
that you love and also have a standard for

00:00:54.750 --> 00:01:00.899
yourself and have some sort of integrity and
try to find some sort of truth in what you're

00:01:00.899 --> 00:01:01.899
doing.

00:01:01.899 --> 00:01:07.799
I think again it goes back to a bit of what
Warren was saying as well, it's the discipline.

00:01:07.799 --> 00:01:10.460
The discipline to not get caught up in the
moment.

00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:12.530
You know music is like stocks too.

00:01:12.530 --> 00:01:15.320
There's the hot thing of the moment.

00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:21.960
There's this hot electro sound or the hot
auto-tune voice or the hot whatever, whatever's

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:24.860
new and exciting.

00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:27.619
People tend to make emotional decisions based
on that.

00:01:27.619 --> 00:01:29.450
They don't stick with what they know.

00:01:29.450 --> 00:01:36.810
This is who I am, this is what I do, and then
they jump on this next hot thing and it's

00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:38.030
not for you.

00:01:38.030 --> 00:01:45.380
So for me, just having the discipline and
having the confidence in who I am and if I

00:01:45.380 --> 00:01:53.030
go into a studio and if I find my truth of
the moment, there are a number of people in

00:01:53.030 --> 00:02:00.770
the world that can relate to what I'm saying
and that's going to buy into what I'm doing.

00:02:00.770 --> 00:02:04.920
Not because it's the new thing of the moment
but because it's my genuine emotions.

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:12.069
It's how I feel, it's how I articulate the
world, just having the discipline to just

00:02:12.069 --> 00:02:13.069
be yourself.

00:02:13.069 --> 00:02:17.920
Yeah, I'm inspired by life and all sorts of
things.

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:22.690
You can say something right now and it would
inspire me to write a song or something to

00:02:22.690 --> 00:02:23.690
happen.

00:02:23.690 --> 00:02:26.640
Most Kings just happened to be inspired by
a Basquiat drawing, you know the drawing he

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:29.890
had that "most young kings get their head
cut off" on the bottom.

00:02:29.890 --> 00:02:35.870
I looked at that and I was like "It's powerful",
just the statement in itself lends itself

00:02:35.870 --> 00:02:37.040
for a song.

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:43.290
The song starts, "Inspired by Basquiat, "my
chariot's on fire, everybody took shots, "hit

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:48.849
my body up, I'm tired, "build me up, break
me down to build me up again.

00:02:48.849 --> 00:02:52.360
"They like, Hov, we need you back so we can
"kill your ass again."

00:02:52.360 --> 00:02:59.260
It's like this thing, this love-hate thing
that the world has with success period.

00:02:59.260 --> 00:03:03.620
It's complex, right, and it's deceiving--

00:03:03.620 --> 00:03:05.280
- [Interviewer] Hm.

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:08.059
- at times, you know, because people think
the two equate.

00:03:08.059 --> 00:03:09.430
- [Interviewer] Yes.

00:03:09.430 --> 00:03:11.030
- You know, to each other and they don't.

00:03:11.030 --> 00:03:13.019
- [Interviewer] Give a lot of money.

00:03:13.019 --> 00:03:14.270
- It's upyeah.

00:03:14.270 --> 00:03:15.270
- And then--

00:03:15.270 --> 00:03:18.050
- That have a lot of happiness but that doesn't
mean that the two equate to each other.

00:03:18.050 --> 00:03:22.120
- [Interviewer] Has your happiness risen at
the same amount as your bank account?

00:03:22.120 --> 00:03:23.790
- No, that's the thing.

00:03:23.790 --> 00:03:24.790
They don't--

00:03:24.790 --> 00:03:25.790
- [Interviewer] They don't equate or--

00:03:25.790 --> 00:03:26.790
- No.

00:03:26.790 --> 00:03:27.790
- [Interviewer] they're not tied in any way
important.

00:03:27.790 --> 00:03:28.790
- They're not tied to each other.

00:03:28.790 --> 00:03:29.790
- [Interviewer] Why?

00:03:29.790 --> 00:03:31.790
- I mean it allows you freedom and it allows
you to go places where you can smile and look

00:03:31.790 --> 00:03:35.520
at the sunset and things like that if that's
what you choose to do and you enjoy to do.

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:36.520
- [Interviewer] Right.

00:03:36.520 --> 00:03:41.180
- But there are a lot of people with tons
of money who are unhappy because either they've

00:03:41.180 --> 00:03:42.599
become a prisoner of their money--

00:03:42.599 --> 00:03:43.599
- [Interviewer] Right.

00:03:43.599 --> 00:03:49.189
- Or they have become so consumed with getting
money that they don't allow time for happiness.

00:03:49.189 --> 00:03:50.189
- [Interviewer] Right.

00:03:50.189 --> 00:03:51.709
- Life is about balance, right?

00:03:51.709 --> 00:03:53.280
You have to have some type of balance.

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:57.930
You have to, like, time for work and there's
time for play and if you don't allow these

00:03:57.930 --> 00:04:02.130
two things to co-exist, you have an imbalance
of one or the other.

00:04:02.130 --> 00:04:06.489
I wish I could say we were geniuses and say
we're going to start our own company.

00:04:06.489 --> 00:04:09.299
That's not what happened.

00:04:09.299 --> 00:04:13.020
In the beginning we went to every single label
and every single label shut their door on

00:04:13.020 --> 00:04:14.730
us.

00:04:14.730 --> 00:04:18.459
The genius thing that we did was we didn't
give up.

00:04:18.459 --> 00:04:25.020
We didn't say because these guys, we used
that what do they know approach.

00:04:25.020 --> 00:04:26.650
We didn't give up at that point.

00:04:26.650 --> 00:04:29.410
I think that was the genius thing we did.

00:04:29.410 --> 00:04:33.030
We started selling our own CDs and we built
our own buzz and then the record company came

00:04:33.030 --> 00:04:38.310
back to us, so now we had a different negotiation.

00:04:38.310 --> 00:04:43.160
It wasn't the same artist-label relationship.

00:04:43.160 --> 00:04:48.759
Now we retain ownership in our own company
and it was the best thing for us.

00:04:48.759 --> 00:04:52.340
Let's not get stuck on one thing.

00:04:52.340 --> 00:04:58.560
You know rap music is about diversity, like
if you got on those Nikes I'm just going to

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:00.100
write, you know, dunks.

00:05:00.100 --> 00:05:05.750
It's all about everyone contribution.

00:05:05.750 --> 00:05:08.370
Before you had Tribe, Ice Cube, Public Enemy.

00:05:08.370 --> 00:05:10.320
At the same time there's Digital Underground.

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:12.639
There was just so many different, and I had
every album.

00:05:12.639 --> 00:05:13.639
- [Interviewer] Um-hmm.

00:05:13.639 --> 00:05:18.470
- It was all different types of sounds and
approaches but it was still great music.

00:05:18.470 --> 00:05:22.310
I'm not just auto-tune is bad, you know.

00:05:22.310 --> 00:05:25.110
There are songs that have auto-tune that I
love.

00:05:25.110 --> 00:05:32.820
But when everyone jumps on this bandwagon
and everyone tries to make the same exact

00:05:32.820 --> 00:05:33.820
record, I mean I heard that record from T-Pain.

00:05:33.820 --> 00:05:34.820
I liked it.

00:05:34.820 --> 00:05:39.151
I don't want to hear it a hundred times from
a hundred different artists.

00:05:39.151 --> 00:05:40.151
I want to hear different stuff.

00:05:40.151 --> 00:05:45.810
Our job is to find that genius-level talent
and then to apply it in the way that supports

00:05:45.810 --> 00:05:59.370
that genius-level talent.

00:05:59.370 --> 00:06:00.370
A quick example is a guy named Earl Manigault
who was said to be way better than Michael

00:06:00.370 --> 00:06:01.370
Jordan but he didn't make the league.

00:06:01.370 --> 00:06:02.370
He was selling drugs.

00:06:02.370 --> 00:06:03.370
He got caught up in drugs.

00:06:03.370 --> 00:06:04.370
His circumstances got in the way.

00:06:04.370 --> 00:06:05.370
So you have to find your genius-level talent.

00:06:05.370 --> 00:06:06.370
Then you have to believe in it so hard because
people will put their shit and their insecurities

00:06:06.370 --> 00:06:07.370
on you.

00:06:07.370 --> 00:06:08.370
- And when I was young I went to my uncle
and had my demo tape, my first demo.

00:06:08.370 --> 00:06:09.370
I was really happy about the shit.

00:06:09.370 --> 00:06:10.370
I played it for him.

00:06:10.370 --> 00:06:11.370
I was like wide-eyed.

00:06:11.370 --> 00:06:20.600
It went off, the first thing he didn't say
keep up the good work.

00:06:20.600 --> 00:06:27.780
He was like man, you ain't going to never
be better than LL Cool J. Now I know you love

00:06:27.780 --> 00:06:34.600
me, I forgive him for it you know, but I think
that something must have happened to him when

00:06:34.600 --> 00:06:39.641
he was trying to pursue his dreams and it
didn't work out for him so he was putting

00:06:39.641 --> 00:06:40.641
that on me.

00:06:40.641 --> 00:06:44.750
So you have to even with people that are close
to you and love you, you have to still have

00:06:44.750 --> 00:06:47.771
so much belief in this genius-level talent
that no matter what anyone says to you, you

00:06:47.771 --> 00:06:54.650
have to have this focus so you have to find
it.

00:06:54.650 --> 00:06:58.780
And you have to believe in it.

00:06:58.780 --> 00:07:05.270
You can't let nobody put their insecurities
and shit on you.

00:07:05.270 --> 00:07:09.400
He just affirmed to me that instincts is really
important in business.

00:07:09.400 --> 00:07:16.960
I didn't go to any proper business school
or read any super, follow any manuals like

00:07:16.960 --> 00:07:19.949
the record business 101 or anything like that.

00:07:19.949 --> 00:07:25.020
I just pretty much followed my instincts and
he just reaffirmed that for me, that your

00:07:25.020 --> 00:07:27.270
instincts are very important to you.

00:07:27.270 --> 00:07:33.789
I love that thing of collaborating, you taking
the best of what you do and someone taking

00:07:33.789 --> 00:07:37.110
the best of what you do and you not robbing
from what they do and they aren't robbing

00:07:37.110 --> 00:07:38.120
from what you do.

00:07:38.120 --> 00:07:42.840
You all bring the best of what you are to
the table and you put it in this mix and you

00:07:42.840 --> 00:07:43.960
see what happens.

00:07:43.960 --> 00:07:52.680
I love that part of creating so there'll definitely
be, I don't believe in those lines that people

00:07:52.680 --> 00:07:56.980
put up for music, you know, this is for this
person, this is for that, this is rock, this

00:07:56.980 --> 00:07:57.980
is blues.

00:07:57.980 --> 00:08:04.370
I just believe good music, bad music and put
those two elements in the room and see what

00:08:04.370 --> 00:08:05.370
happens.

00:08:05.370 --> 00:08:14.771
Let God and the universe, the movement, whatever
happens in that room, we, not we, I. Whatever

00:08:14.771 --> 00:08:16.750
happens, make great music.

00:08:16.750 --> 00:08:25.539
When I started this Shawn Carter Scholarship
Program, noticing how education was not being

00:08:25.539 --> 00:08:30.990
addressed in our neighborhood and also as
a way to show people in our neighborhood that

00:08:30.990 --> 00:08:37.520
you can be successful and come back, that
that person that use to live in 5C at 534

00:08:37.520 --> 00:08:43.320
Flushing is not a person in a box now, not
someone you just see at a concert or see on

00:08:43.320 --> 00:08:44.540
TV.

00:08:44.540 --> 00:08:50.260
Most people that were successful growing up
from where I was from never came back.

00:08:50.260 --> 00:08:54.500
So there was never a dialogue on how'd you
do it, what happened.

00:08:54.500 --> 00:08:56.310
There was no mentoring program.

00:08:56.310 --> 00:09:03.470
There was no going back and grabbing a person
and teaching them a trade or what it is that

00:09:03.470 --> 00:09:08.320
you do and then that person go back and grab
two people and then, you know.

00:09:08.320 --> 00:09:09.910
It goes from there.

00:09:09.910 --> 00:09:14.510
So I figured I'd start with school because
education--

00:09:14.510 --> 00:09:17.790
- [Interviewer] It's the answer.

00:09:17.790 --> 00:09:18.790
- It's the answer.

00:09:18.790 --> 00:09:19.920
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:24.320
I made this video because a bunch of you were
asking for it so if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:09:24.320 --> 00:09:27.921
that you want me to profile next, leave it
in the comments below and I'll see what I

00:09:27.921 --> 00:09:28.921
can do.

00:09:28.921 --> 00:09:33.440
I'd also love to know which of Jay Z's top
ten rules most resonated with you.

00:09:33.440 --> 00:09:34.440
Leave it in the comments.

00:09:34.440 --> 00:09:36.380
I'm going to join in the discussion.

00:09:36.380 --> 00:09:37.450
Thank you so much for watching.

00:09:37.450 --> 00:09:40.310
Continue to believe and I'll see you soon.

